!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/meta-http-3.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta-kernel.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta-kernel.tn"
@doc:"""
  The leaner operation: four fields, no enums, no nested records, no lists. Resolves as written;
  applying it is blocked on UPSTREAM.md #15, like meta-http-1.tn.

  Compared with meta-http-1.tn this trades away three things and is smaller for it.

    - `method: text` rather than an http_method enum. Any string is a method; the enum bought
      validation of a closed set, which a description arguably does not need.
    - `type_name` rather than `type_ref`. A name, with no generic arguments -- so an operation names
      `order_page`, and what `order_page` is (an application of page<order>) is that entry's business.
    - ONE `response`, not a list of status-and-body pairs. Multi-status becomes a named choice type
      in the schema, which is what it is semantically. The operation stays a 4-tuple.

  The last of those is the real trade. The operation becomes trivially readable -- path, method,
  request, response, four fields, no traversal -- but the status-to-body mapping moves down into the
  response type, so a consumer that wants statuses still walks the choice and its variants. It does
  not remove that walk; it removes it from the OPERATION.

  AND THIS IS PROBABLY THE WRONG CHOICE, for a reason that only shows up in the resolver.

  A reference in a constructor payload is resolved by DefinitionResolver.takesATypeRef, which decides
  by the SLOT'S DECLARED TYPE -- is this field a `type_ref`? -- rather than by knowing which meta
  records have reference slots. It follows aliases, and says why: "a meta layer may name the kernel's
  own type_ref something of its own". So the mechanism is generic and was written for exactly this.

  It tests for `type_ref`. There is no counterpart for `type_name`, which is resolved only where the
  resolver has specific handling, as in `supertypes`. So meta-http-1.tn's `request: type_ref` should
  be checked and this file's `request: type_name` most likely is not -- which would make it
  api-1.tn's `type: text` in better clothing.

  Kept because the shape is worth comparing, but prefer meta-http-1.tn. Untestable either way until
  UPSTREAM.md #15 lets a user-defined constructor be applied at all.
  """
{
  operation => ~top & {
    path:     text
    method:   text
    request:  type_name
    response: type_name
  }
}
